1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It covers 2,400 miles and more than 75 years of history.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:13,000
It's the mother road, the Will Rogers Highway, and the Main Street of America.

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:23,000
From the shores of Lake Michigan to the Santa Monica Pier, it's a ribbon of concrete, steel, and kitch.

4
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, Route 66 on Modern Marvels.

5
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,600
Travel my way, take the highway, that's the best.

6
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:10,400
Get your kicks on Route 66.

7
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:18,580
Route 66 was for decades, starting in 1926, the main road to the Pacific.

8
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:25,560
A patchwork of trails, roads, and main streets cobbled together under the umbrella of an interstate highway

9
00:01:25,561 --> 00:01:28,420
that traveled three quarters the way across the country.

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,200
But Route 66 has always been more than the sum of its parts, more than just a way for a car to travel from Chicago to Los Angeles.

11
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:41,040
It embodies an idea central to the American myth.

12
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:44,340
The belief that something better is just over the horizon.

13
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:59,480
So people like me, writers and poets and musicians and photographers and artists and even dancers have interpreted Route 66.

14
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
It's a metaphor for all of these other roads.

15
00:02:05,001 --> 00:02:08,000
Today it exists as a symbol.

16
00:02:08,001 --> 00:02:12,000
The route was decommissioned when super highways moved more people faster.

17
00:02:12,001 --> 00:02:18,000
But that symbol still sends a searching for vestiges of what author John Steinbeck called the Mother Road,

18
00:02:18,001 --> 00:02:24,000
along stretches of old concrete, over abandoned bridges,

19
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:29,000
in rusted signs welcoming ghosts to faded diners,

20
00:02:29,001 --> 00:02:30,700
auto camps, and motels.

21
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:33,040
It was a 2,400-mile carnival.

22
00:02:34,040 --> 00:02:37,220
The barbecue pits with all this wonderful aroma

23
00:02:37,221 --> 00:02:39,540
wafting through the car.

24
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:45,520
The diners, the motels with their wild neon,

25
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:47,100
that just looked so great.

26
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:49,900
There was something almost a line every inch

27
00:02:49,901 --> 00:02:51,140
to get your down.

28
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:55,120
Because in those days, most people were driving to California to stay.

29
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:56,620
They figured they got one shot at you.

30
00:02:56,621 --> 00:03:02,620
That cross-country carnival celebrated technology that became state-of-the-art

31
00:03:02,621 --> 00:03:08,620
for paving, bridge building, and traveling itself.

32
00:03:08,621 --> 00:03:15,620
Even though the roads that made up Route 66 existed long before the government marked and posted the road with signs,

33
00:03:15,621 --> 00:03:21,620
throughout its existence, it generated engineering projects that kept traffic moving and men working.

34
00:03:21,621 --> 00:03:26,620
Its days as an interstate are gone, but the road carries on as living,

35
00:03:26,621 --> 00:03:31,260
breathing nostalgia, a place you can still visit if you're willing to get off the super highway.

36
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:38,009
On Route 66, the future is the past.

37
00:03:38,010 --> 00:03:41,980
Welcome to the Route 66 Museum in Pontiac, Illinois,

38
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:47,380
and the Route 66 Museum in McLean, Texas.

39
00:03:47,381 --> 00:03:54,060
And the vintage independent gas station in Monrovia, California.

40
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:58,620
Stop by the Blue Swallow Motel.

41
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:04,200
The Route 66 drive-in theater.

42
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:11,120
Or Kuku drive-in restaurant.

43
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:17,220
Stroll through Main Street Galena.

44
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:23,320
These places and others like them are beacons, not just to Americans, but visitors from all over the world.

45
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:28,460
There you go. Thank you very much.

46
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:40,018
They're coming to see the real America, and there's nowhere in the rest of the world that you can package the real America, let alone Route 66, that wonderful

47
00:04:40,019 --> 00:04:41,600
2,400-mile museum now.

48
00:04:44,940 --> 00:04:54,420
Route 66 starts in Chicago, but it really began in the early 20th century, when a confluence of technologies merged with a set of compelling facts.

49
00:04:54,421 --> 00:04:57,980
The technologies are familiar enough.

50
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:01,220
The automobile invented in the late 19th century

51
00:05:01,221 --> 00:05:03,620
was being transformed from an expensive novelty

52
00:05:03,621 --> 00:05:05,020
into a necessary tool.

53
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:07,520
So as the car developed

54
00:05:07,521 --> 00:05:11,860
and as people like Ford made the automobile affordable

55
00:05:11,861 --> 00:05:14,200
for the common person, for the clerk,

56
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:15,700
for the school teacher, for the former,

57
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:17,820
and not just a play toy of the rich,

58
00:05:17,821 --> 00:05:19,440
we need it more roads.

59
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:24,140
Auto enthusiasts pressured Congress for better roads.

60
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:27,120
But the lure of interstate commerce convinced them.

61
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:33,200
Roads allowed trucks to move goods from places railroads couldn't easily reach.

62
00:05:33,820 --> 00:05:37,460
Small farms and remote communities across the Midwest and the West.

63
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:42,500
Rural towns wanted big city goods and vice versa.

64
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:46,540
Two of the biggest cities were Chicago and Los Angeles.

65
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:50,180
Los Angeles was the major shipping port on the west coast.

66
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:54,400
And Chicago was really the furthest inland that any boats could get from the Atlantic Ocean.

67
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:55,920
side via the Great Lakes.

68
00:05:55,921 --> 00:06:00,420
So the choice between Chicago and Los Angeles was primarily a commerce rap.

69
00:06:00,421 --> 00:06:08,420
Congress passed a series of acts in the teens and 20s, making funds available to states for creating and improving roads.

70
00:06:08,421 --> 00:06:12,290
The first was the Federal Road Act of 1916.

71
00:06:12,291 --> 00:06:17,420
But more significant was the Federal Highway Act of 1921,

72
00:06:17,421 --> 00:06:22,172
which offered funds for interstate highways. The need was great.

73
00:06:22,173 --> 00:06:24,400
In 1920, only 36,000 more than

74
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:27,420
miles of America's roads were negotiable by automobile.

75
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:33,420
Some were paved, most were dirt.

76
00:06:33,421 --> 00:06:35,420
Some of them were old Indian trails.

77
00:06:35,421 --> 00:06:39,420
So when the car came along, a lot of the roads were not car-friendly.

78
00:06:39,421 --> 00:06:41,420
The roads were terribly congested with all these cars.

79
00:06:41,421 --> 00:06:44,420
And if you think about it, there weren't just cars, there were trucks,

80
00:06:44,421 --> 00:06:47,420
there were still horse-drawn vehicles, there were buses.

81
00:06:49,420 --> 00:06:53,420
A planned series of major routes from city to city across state borders

82
00:06:53,421 --> 00:06:54,720
spied across the country.

83
00:06:55,720 --> 00:07:00,860
These interstate highways maintained a single ID, or route number, from one state to the next,

84
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:03,220
codifying a national system of roads.

85
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:12,220
In 1923, Oklahoma Highway Commissioner Cyrus Avery was named consulting highway specialist

86
00:07:12,221 --> 00:07:14,520
to the United States Bureau of Public Roads.

87
00:07:15,220 --> 00:07:18,720
He was instrumental in turning federal funds into drivable roadways.

88
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:23,400
The goal of Avery and his colleagues was not necessarily to build

89
00:07:23,420 --> 00:07:29,540
new roads, but to improve existing ones connecting urban and rural areas.

90
00:07:29,541 --> 00:07:34,020
Avery's committee chose as one of its most important transcontinental conduits, a highway

91
00:07:34,021 --> 00:07:38,800
that began in Chicago and followed a route between the Northern Santa Fe Trail and the Southern

92
00:07:38,801 --> 00:07:41,700
Butterfield Stage Line to the Pacific.

93
00:07:41,701 --> 00:07:45,880
The route would take travelers through territory that was fewer than 50 years earlier,

94
00:07:45,881 --> 00:07:49,300
inhabited by Native Americans' frontier boom towns and Buffalo.

95
00:07:49,301 --> 00:07:56,300
It was a series of trails and traces historic, many of them very, very old.

96
00:07:56,301 --> 00:08:00,300
They had been well-used roads, familiar roads, so it was a no-brainer.

97
00:08:00,301 --> 00:08:03,300
Let's connect these roads.

98
00:08:03,301 --> 00:08:07,300
Heading south out of Illinois, the route turned due west in Oklahoma,

99
00:08:07,301 --> 00:08:12,300
with the goal of sending trucks loaded with goods through relatively temperate southern climes.

100
00:08:12,301 --> 00:08:18,300
But Avery, who is sometimes called the father of Route 66, may have had an ulterior motive.

101
00:08:18,301 --> 00:08:24,580
I don't think it's a coincidence that he had a business on Route 66, but he definitely wanted it in his hometown.

102
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:29,480
He definitely wanted to boost Oklahoma, and he was, you know, willing to fight like hell to get it.

103
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:36,320
Avery's committee originally wanted to call the road Route 60, but another route back east got the name first.

104
00:08:36,860 --> 00:08:40,060
So the committee came up with Route 66, thinking it might be catchy.

105
00:08:40,840 --> 00:08:42,640
And finally, it became this reality.

106
00:08:42,641 --> 00:08:51,280
In November of 1926, we had Route 66 officially born and created and ready to go to work.

107
00:08:55,840 --> 00:09:01,280
Illinois was one of only two states where every mile of Route 66 was already paved at its inception.

108
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:09,120
Only 800 of the more than 2,400 miles of Route 66 were paved in 1926.

109
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:19,640
The route began in Chicago to facilitate commerce coming off Lake Michigan and nearby railroads.

110
00:09:19,641 --> 00:09:22,640
But it was more than just a commerce route.

111
00:09:22,641 --> 00:09:30,640
People were captivated by new vehicles, new cars, and new roads in the mid-1920s, and Route 66 embodied that giddy time.

112
00:09:30,641 --> 00:09:36,640
From Lake Michigan's shore past Al Capone's town of Cicero, through Abe Lincoln's Springfield.

113
00:09:36,641 --> 00:09:39,640
Route 66 travels over prairie.

114
00:09:39,641 --> 00:09:41,640
It undoubtedly served as a whiskey route for Boothoombe.

115
00:09:41,641 --> 00:09:45,540
a whiskey route for bootleggers moving pooch from Canada into speakeas

116
00:09:45,541 --> 00:09:53,200
throughout the Midwest during the late 1920s but when Route 66 reaches the

117
00:09:53,201 --> 00:09:58,740
Missouri border it hits a hurdle the Mississippi River route 66 needed an

118
00:09:58,741 --> 00:10:06,420
engineering marvel to overcome this barrier on May 26 1928 Andy Payne of

119
00:10:06,421 --> 00:10:11,620
Foyle Oklahoma won the first and only Bunyan Derby a foot race along

120
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:14,040
Route 66 from Los Angeles to Chicago.

121
00:10:14,920 --> 00:10:17,400
Route 66 will return on Modern Marvels.

122
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:23,540
We now return to Route 66 on Modern Marbles.

123
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:31,940
North of St. Louis, near where the Mississippi and Missouri rivers join,

124
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:40,440
is an old bridge that was from 1936 to 1968, the main gateway to Missouri to Missouri for travelers along Route 66.

125
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:45,980
These days, it's open only to pedestrians.

126
00:10:45,981 --> 00:10:51,960
But not too long ago, it was packed with vehicles crossing the river.

127
00:10:55,920 --> 00:11:01,140
The chain of rocks bridge has become one of the key landmarks along old Route 66.

128
00:11:01,141 --> 00:11:06,160
But it wasn't initially even part of the route when the highway was commissioned in 1926.

129
00:11:06,161 --> 00:11:09,540
It was adopted in 1936.

130
00:11:09,541 --> 00:11:15,660
its nearly 60-year history, Route 66 underwent numerous realignments, alterations in its path

131
00:11:15,661 --> 00:11:19,820
that often, though not always, improved its ability to send traffic where it needed to go.

132
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:26,193
From almost the moment that the highway became official, changes were begun.

133
00:11:26,194 --> 00:11:27,480
Dangerous curves

134
00:11:27,481 --> 00:11:29,813
were taken out. Bridges were replaced.

135
00:11:29,814 --> 00:11:32,760
Railroad crossings were given grade separations,

136
00:11:33,020 --> 00:11:36,120
and so the highway immediately began to change.

137
00:11:36,121 --> 00:11:39,420
Early realignments occurred in Missouri,

138
00:11:39,860 --> 00:11:42,860
where the road took traffic straight through the heart of bustling St. Louis.

139
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:51,680
Meanwhile, north of the city, a private consortium was planning a toll bridge across the Mississippi.

140
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:56,160
The idea seemed simple enough, but to the engineers of this new span,

141
00:11:56,460 --> 00:12:00,020
it must have been one nightmare after another, beginning with the flow of the river.

142
00:12:00,260 --> 00:12:04,440
The velocity of the chain of rocks was one of the highest in the entire river.

143
00:12:04,441 --> 00:12:08,900
that makes it tougher to build piers with the water moving so quickly.

144
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:11,660
So it's just not a very good spot to put a bridge.

145
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:14,940
That was only the beginning of the complications.

146
00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:18,640
The consortium had already purchased land on both sides of the river,

147
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:22,180
dictating that the bridge would have to cross at an angle to the river channel.

148
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:26,020
Not an ideal situation from a navigation perspective.

149
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:31,920
But prominent city waterworks intake towers just downstream,

150
00:12:31,921 --> 00:12:36,280
compelled river pilots to insist on a radical alteration to the planned path.

151
00:12:38,060 --> 00:12:40,180
If the bridge had been put in as was originally planned,

152
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:44,700
they would have had multiple obstacles to navigation.

153
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:48,960
Now, they ended up convincing the War Department,

154
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:51,580
which in those days gave the permits for the bridge,

155
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:55,340
to require that the main navigation span of the bridge be placed

156
00:12:55,341 --> 00:12:57,940
basically square to the navigation channel.

157
00:12:57,941 --> 00:13:01,900
And also in the process, the center pier of the main bridge,

158
00:13:01,920 --> 00:13:06,920
would shield and protect somewhat the water intake tower.

159
00:13:06,921 --> 00:13:12,920
The only way to resolve the navigation dilemma was to build part of the bridge square to the navigation channel,

160
00:13:12,921 --> 00:13:16,920
then turn it toward the shore, where the consortium had its property.

161
00:13:16,921 --> 00:13:20,920
The Chain of Rock's Bridge was designed with a 22-degree dog leg.

162
00:13:20,921 --> 00:13:26,920
Then there was the challenge of sinking coffer dams, large, hollow, watertight, reinforced boxes

163
00:13:26,921 --> 00:13:30,920
that are excavated into the riverbed to build the bridge piers.

164
00:13:30,921 --> 00:13:43,920
Since the piers are going down to bedrock, you are relying on the embedment of the coffer dam in the soil above the bedrock to keep it stable.

165
00:13:43,921 --> 00:13:50,920
And when you have high velocity, the Mississippi River scours a lot, and it is possible to scour down to bedrock.

166
00:13:50,921 --> 00:13:56,920
The force of the river is so strong here, it literally strips the bottom soil away.

167
00:13:56,921 --> 00:13:58,920
That's scouring.

168
00:13:58,921 --> 00:14:02,100
And that could lead to the failure of the coffer dam

169
00:14:02,101 --> 00:14:05,340
because there's nothing really restraining it at that point.

170
00:14:05,341 --> 00:14:08,180
Engineers overcame the challenges the river posed,

171
00:14:08,181 --> 00:14:11,520
and the rest of the bridge was built without incident.

172
00:14:11,521 --> 00:14:13,760
The steel support structure went up efficiently,

173
00:14:13,761 --> 00:14:15,300
reinforced near the center of the bridge

174
00:14:15,301 --> 00:14:18,200
to give it greater rigidity.

175
00:14:18,201 --> 00:14:21,360
When completed, the bridge was more than a mile long.

176
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:28,900
More memorably, it was only 20 feet wide.

177
00:14:28,920 --> 00:14:31,160
and felt even narrower at the bend.

178
00:14:31,161 --> 00:14:34,920
I can remember in a couple occasions during an ice storm

179
00:14:34,921 --> 00:14:37,920
with my father crossing that bridge in a sedan

180
00:14:37,921 --> 00:14:41,920
and feeling sort of like a hockey puck as we hit that dog leg

181
00:14:41,921 --> 00:14:45,920
and then we're careening over towards the Illinois bank

182
00:14:45,921 --> 00:14:49,920
and here come trucks up the road and it's a narrow bridge.

183
00:14:49,921 --> 00:14:55,920
It was just those two lanes of danger over the Mississippi.

184
00:14:55,921 --> 00:14:56,920
Mississippi.

185
00:14:56,921 --> 00:15:05,920
The private consortium that built the bridge had the misfortune of opening it on the eve of the Great Depression.

186
00:15:05,921 --> 00:15:09,920
There were two few five-cent tolls collected to keep them in business.

187
00:15:09,921 --> 00:15:14,920
But the Missouri Department of Transportation saw in the bridge an opportunity.

188
00:15:14,921 --> 00:15:21,920
Highway engineers sought to realign interstate traffic away from the hub of and congestion downtown,

189
00:15:21,921 --> 00:15:24,920
using the now bankrupt chain of Rocks Bridge.

190
00:15:24,921 --> 00:15:32,920
The bridge remained the official Route 66 crossing for three decades, until this part of the highway was decommissioned in 1968.

191
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:45,920
Missouri's section of Route 66 followed the historic Great Osage Trail, an Indian trading route, and passed through towns such as Springfield and Joplin.

192
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:53,920
But south of Rala, a realignment called Hooker Cut, made use of cutting-edge technology, literally.

193
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:59,400
Road engineers sliced right through the mountainside.

194
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:04,420
Picker cut, actually, about its deepest point, is about 93-foot cut into the mountain.

195
00:16:05,080 --> 00:16:11,240
It's 86-foot wide, and there's 186,000 cubic yards approximately of a rock that came out of that cut.

196
00:16:11,800 --> 00:16:15,320
Now, that 186,000 cubic yards of rock weighed probably around 300,000 tons.

197
00:16:15,980 --> 00:16:19,820
And to give you an idea of the size of the volume of that material coming out,

198
00:16:19,821 --> 00:16:22,600
if you were to take that material and place it down on a football field,

199
00:16:22,601 --> 00:16:27,180
and spread across the football field, it would actually stack to be over 100 foot tall.

200
00:16:27,680 --> 00:16:31,100
Hooker Cut proved to be one of the most dramatic realignments of Route 66.

201
00:16:31,780 --> 00:16:36,060
The need for improved access to nearby Fort Leonard Wood necessitated the realignment.

202
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:39,960
And you needed a way to move troops quickly to the new base.

203
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:46,000
So the Missouri State Highway Department decided to build a four-lane road leading to the military base.

204
00:16:46,760 --> 00:16:52,540
It's about 22 feet wide on either side with two 11-foot lanes, whereas the original road was only 19 feet wide.

205
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:55,720
and crossed a bridge that was, again, only 19 feet wide

206
00:16:55,721 --> 00:16:57,020
with about a 12-foot clearance.

207
00:16:57,440 --> 00:16:59,720
So there was no way a military vehicle

208
00:16:59,721 --> 00:17:01,560
or any military equipment could be moved

209
00:17:01,561 --> 00:17:02,760
to the military base.

210
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,080
Cutting straight through the Ozark landscape

211
00:17:07,081 --> 00:17:09,240
rather than following its natural contours

212
00:17:09,241 --> 00:17:10,420
was a formidable task.

213
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:13,760
In order to create a new and improved 66,

214
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:15,820
the St. Louis-based contractor

215
00:17:15,821 --> 00:17:17,480
had to take his unwieldy equipment

216
00:17:17,481 --> 00:17:20,160
over the old narrow route to deliver it to the site.

217
00:17:20,440 --> 00:17:21,880
They used steam-powered drills

218
00:17:21,881 --> 00:17:27,260
to drill fissure holes into the rock and then placed dynamite and explosives down in there

219
00:17:27,261 --> 00:17:30,300
and literally blow apart the mountain in order to cut through it.

220
00:17:33,640 --> 00:17:36,380
It took almost two years to complete this process.

221
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:44,360
Despite these difficulties, Booker Cut broke ground, not only physically, but also in highway design.

222
00:17:45,300 --> 00:17:48,560
This rock cut here was designed with a bench and ended up the top there.

223
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:50,200
That's a standard practice nowadays.

224
00:17:50,201 --> 00:17:55,620
we'll go up 30 foot and then we'll cut back in 15 foot as for a bench and then go up another 30 foot

225
00:17:55,621 --> 00:18:00,320
and that is because if a piece of rock or something like that from the very top of there was to fall down and hit a car down here

226
00:18:00,321 --> 00:18:09,504
that rock will fall down and hit that bench versus coming all the way down the hill this project became one of the first four lane sections of route 66 it would

227
00:18:09,505 --> 00:18:19,089
incorporate new and largely untested design features such as a median strip to separate traffic traveling in different directions and a beveled curb which was intended

228
00:18:19,090 --> 00:18:20,180
to sluice rainwater

229
00:18:20,200 --> 00:18:21,200
toward gutters.

230
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:30,600
Upon completion in 1946, Hooker Cut became the deepest limestone cut in Missouri, and possibly the entire country.

231
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:37,400
Though it would take years before improvements like cuts and divided highways were widespread,

232
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:40,600
they would ultimately doom winding old Route 66.

233
00:18:40,800 --> 00:18:45,400
Because of that, they were able to eventually build roads such as Interstate 44,

234
00:18:45,401 --> 00:18:49,800
which is just to the north of here, without having to follow the topography of the land.

235
00:18:50,200 --> 00:18:56,200
After leaving Missouri, Route 66 wanders for 13 miles through Kansas.

236
00:18:56,201 --> 00:19:01,200
The only other state besides Illinois who have paved its portion of the highway by 1926.

237
00:19:01,201 --> 00:19:11,200
As the United States entered the 1930s, Route 66 was about to become the most important road in America.

238
00:19:11,201 --> 00:19:20,200
In 1935, Lester Dill opened Missouri's Merrimack Caverns as a road

239
00:19:20,201 --> 00:19:25,440
side attraction, promoting it with billboards and painted barns along Route 66.

240
00:19:25,441 --> 00:19:30,440
He even placed ads on visitors' cards and is credited with inventing the bumper sticker.

241
00:19:30,441 --> 00:19:35,820
Route 66 will return on Modern Marvels.

242
00:19:35,821 --> 00:19:42,080
We now return to Route 66 on Modern Marvels.

243
00:19:42,081 --> 00:19:47,120
Miami, Oklahoma is home to a stretch of rare and authentic Route 66.

244
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:52,440
It's actually older than the route itself.

245
00:19:52,441 --> 00:19:57,140
It's earned these various nicknames, like the Scotch Highway,

246
00:19:57,141 --> 00:19:58,940
or the ribbon road.

247
00:19:58,941 --> 00:20:04,040
My favorite name, it's the sidewalk highway.

248
00:20:04,041 --> 00:20:06,180
This roughly four miles stretch of road

249
00:20:06,181 --> 00:20:09,380
may be one of the rarest pieces of original 66 surface

250
00:20:09,381 --> 00:20:12,920
along the entire route, but it's still enduring obvious wear and tear.

251
00:20:14,920 --> 00:20:17,560
More people know what I'm talking about when I say sidewalk highway.

252
00:20:17,561 --> 00:20:20,100
They know where it is, they can find it.

253
00:20:20,200 --> 00:20:23,500
One thing that saved this particular part is that there are a lot of farms out here,

254
00:20:23,501 --> 00:20:26,200
and this is really the only access that people have to their land.

255
00:20:26,201 --> 00:20:33,200
Port in 1922, this piece of original roadway is only nine feet across.

256
00:20:33,201 --> 00:20:37,700
In those days, when the state received federal funds to build and improve roads,

257
00:20:37,701 --> 00:20:42,700
it cost a whopping $40,000 per mile to pave an 18-foot-wide track.

258
00:20:44,700 --> 00:20:49,200
Legend has it, thrifty engineers determined if they built their road only half as wide.

259
00:20:49,201 --> 00:20:51,060
They could pave it twice as far.

260
00:20:51,740 --> 00:20:54,420
Nine foot, it was even by 1920 standards,

261
00:20:54,540 --> 00:20:56,680
would not have been a two-lane paved road.

262
00:20:57,040 --> 00:20:58,740
It would have been a one-lane paved road.

263
00:20:59,280 --> 00:21:00,520
Undoubtedly, at the time,

264
00:21:00,640 --> 00:21:02,360
that decision was driven by the desire

265
00:21:02,361 --> 00:21:04,460
to have an all-weathered road to get up out of the mud

266
00:21:04,461 --> 00:21:06,260
and be able to go from one town to the other.

267
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:08,760
Well, at least it was paved.

268
00:21:09,600 --> 00:21:11,540
During its early, more adventurous years,

269
00:21:11,700 --> 00:21:13,640
whole sections of Route 66 warrant.

270
00:21:14,740 --> 00:21:16,900
In fact, all sorts of unorthodox surfaces

271
00:21:16,901 --> 00:21:18,420
were considered good enough.

272
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:21,000
Well, we had three different versions of dirt.

273
00:21:21,540 --> 00:21:22,940
We had just dirt highways,

274
00:21:23,060 --> 00:21:27,020
and we had what's in our archives termed as improved dirt and oil dirt.

275
00:21:27,420 --> 00:21:28,860
We would have gravel highways.

276
00:21:29,640 --> 00:21:34,640
By the mid-teens, more than 2,000 U.S. roads were surfaced with Portland cement.

277
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:38,720
During the 1930s, efforts were underway to pave Route 66,

278
00:21:39,260 --> 00:21:40,920
as well as all of the nation's interstates.

279
00:21:41,340 --> 00:21:46,220
And by 1938, all of Route 66 was paved, much of it with Portland cement.

280
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:48,120
It goes down quick.

281
00:21:48,121 --> 00:21:51,060
and materials for it are easy to obtain for the most part.

282
00:21:51,360 --> 00:21:53,440
However, a lot of things factored in.

283
00:21:53,480 --> 00:21:56,780
It depended on what kind of budget a local jurisdiction was working with.

284
00:21:57,660 --> 00:22:01,960
Back in the 30s, paving Route 66 was no different than paving any other roads.

285
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:07,940
Pouring a cement roadbed usually involved placing the mixture right on the underlying soil.

286
00:22:08,740 --> 00:22:13,880
We used wooden forms to hold the sides of the concrete up, mixed the concrete on site,

287
00:22:14,560 --> 00:22:17,080
they deposited in front of the paving screen.

288
00:22:18,120 --> 00:22:22,460
discrete or leveling board following behind would level the surface.

289
00:22:22,461 --> 00:22:26,220
They were lucky they had a type of mixture that actually

290
00:22:26,221 --> 00:22:30,220
just rode up on the forms ahead of where the paving operation was going on

291
00:22:30,221 --> 00:22:33,640
and they had a little boom system. Once they mixed the concrete they could

292
00:22:33,641 --> 00:22:37,740
dump it into a bucket and pull it out on a boom and then deposit it where they

293
00:22:37,741 --> 00:22:39,000
wanted to.

294
00:22:39,001 --> 00:22:42,740
Pulling up the rear, men with trowels would do the final finishing.

295
00:22:45,680 --> 00:22:48,100
The only other challenge to laying a concrete

296
00:22:48,120 --> 00:22:52,566
roadway with sufficient manpower. The process was labor intensive.

297
00:22:52,567 --> 00:22:54,520
But as efforts to pave all of

298
00:22:54,521 --> 00:23:01,040
Route 66 gathered steam throughout the late 1920s and early 1930s, America was gripped by a devastating

299
00:23:01,041 --> 00:23:04,559
financial collapse that threw millions of men out of work.

300
00:23:04,560 --> 00:23:06,440
The disastrous human conditions

301
00:23:06,441 --> 00:23:11,480
brought on by the Great Depression produced an endless supply of cheap labor as migrants searching

302
00:23:11,481 --> 00:23:14,094
for a better life found work on the road.

303
00:23:14,095 --> 00:23:17,920
I'd talked to an old man out in western Oklahoma, long gone,

304
00:23:18,120 --> 00:23:23,120
who was a young farmer with a young wife on what became Route 66.

305
00:23:23,121 --> 00:23:28,120
He remembered the lane of the concrete, the crude machinery,

306
00:23:28,121 --> 00:23:34,120
the men, many of them migrants who stopped to get a job, long, long hours.

307
00:23:34,121 --> 00:23:37,120
And every night he'd get his young wife,

308
00:23:37,121 --> 00:23:41,120
and they'd take a wind-up vichrola and some other young couples,

309
00:23:41,121 --> 00:23:43,120
and they'd go out to the new highway,

310
00:23:43,121 --> 00:23:47,120
and they would turn the music on, and they would dance on the road.

311
00:23:47,121 --> 00:23:48,019
on the road.

312
00:23:48,020 --> 00:23:54,460
And they did that all the way across western Oklahoma, until the road went into Texas.

313
00:23:54,960 --> 00:23:56,900
They danced on the mother road.

314
00:23:58,640 --> 00:24:01,035
Many of those young farmers became migrants.

315
00:24:01,036 --> 00:24:04,900
After years of poor farming techniques aggravated by a hellish drought,

316
00:24:05,320 --> 00:24:08,203
crippled farm communities throughout the Midwest.

317
00:24:08,204 --> 00:24:11,440
As depleted topsoil was blown away by dry summer winds,

318
00:24:11,441 --> 00:24:15,320
it created a dust bowl, where once there had been bountiful crops.

319
00:24:17,120 --> 00:24:25,120
These two disasters conspired to drive many tenant farmers off land they'd worked for generations.

320
00:24:25,121 --> 00:24:30,120
The rain literally stopped.

321
00:24:30,121 --> 00:24:37,120
It was just a terrible time in America's breadbasket.

322
00:24:37,121 --> 00:24:46,120
Right on the heart of this new highway of Route 66, that's where you had the oakeies and the folks from the heart of the desolate.

323
00:24:46,121 --> 00:24:51,420
the heart of the Dust Bowl itself, taking to the road in large numbers, legions of people,

324
00:24:51,820 --> 00:24:54,880
the largest migration we've ever had in this country.

325
00:24:55,840 --> 00:25:01,080
Route 66 became the conduit for more than 200,000 of these migrants, headed for California.

326
00:25:01,780 --> 00:25:06,340
That's when the road symbolically started to roll up its sleeves.

327
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:08,620
This is when it started paying its dues.

328
00:25:09,820 --> 00:25:14,680
Author John Steinbeck immortalized their journey in his classic novel, The Grapes of Wrath.

329
00:25:14,681 --> 00:25:21,800
The road that it once captivated with a promise of something better, now compelled with the reality that things could get no worse.

330
00:25:22,380 --> 00:25:27,080
That's when Route 66, Steinbeck so aptly named it became the Mother Road.

331
00:25:27,380 --> 00:25:31,500
For the Oakees who traveled that road, it became a road of promise.

332
00:25:32,340 --> 00:25:43,680
Here they are in these old cars with tires about as thin as cigarette paper, with all their lives and hopes lashed to this vehicle.

333
00:25:43,681 --> 00:25:46,120
and they're traveling west.

334
00:25:46,820 --> 00:25:49,240
They're following the scent of the orange blossoms,

335
00:25:49,880 --> 00:25:51,780
the smell of the Pacific surf.

336
00:25:52,900 --> 00:25:54,980
And they're hoping to rebuild their lives.

337
00:25:56,280 --> 00:25:58,680
Many clutched mass-produced handbills

338
00:25:58,681 --> 00:26:01,160
that promised agricultural work in California.

339
00:26:03,040 --> 00:26:04,580
Many were turned away,

340
00:26:05,080 --> 00:26:07,360
or were even beaten by local vigilantes.

341
00:26:09,400 --> 00:26:12,700
Some simply died of despair by the side of Route 66.

342
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:20,680
As the route straightened due west through towns like Catusa, Hydro, and Elk City, Oklahoma,

343
00:26:20,681 --> 00:26:30,680
and into the Texas panhandle, it passed by general stores, diners, and gas stations, oases in the otherwise poverty-stricken communities of the Heartland.

344
00:26:30,681 --> 00:26:34,680
A service industry grew up along Route 66.

345
00:26:34,681 --> 00:26:39,680
Waitresses and mechanics watched the desperate and the hungry drift by.

346
00:26:39,681 --> 00:26:41,680
They needed food.

347
00:26:41,681 --> 00:26:42,680
They needed gas.

348
00:26:42,681 --> 00:26:47,680
needed gas and their pennies sustained businesses along the way.

349
00:26:47,681 --> 00:26:53,680
They were fleeing from starvation and indebtedness.

350
00:26:53,681 --> 00:26:57,680
These were people that had to find a promised lander they were going to buy.

351
00:26:57,681 --> 00:27:04,680
And of course that come across to the people that served and that's what provided the heart to help those people.

352
00:27:04,681 --> 00:27:06,680
Not everyone helped.

353
00:27:06,681 --> 00:27:09,680
Vigilantes and bandits plagued travelers.

354
00:27:09,681 --> 00:27:11,680
Route 66 threw a punch as easily as it again.

355
00:27:11,681 --> 00:27:13,680
as easily as it extended a hand.

356
00:27:13,681 --> 00:27:15,680
But enough did help.

357
00:27:15,681 --> 00:27:18,680
Attendance, waitresses, and campsite operators.

358
00:27:18,681 --> 00:27:21,680
Some even made their careers assisting travelers.

359
00:27:21,681 --> 00:27:30,680
In McLean, Texas, the very first Phillips 66 gas station in the Lone Star State

360
00:27:30,681 --> 00:27:34,680
is also one of the earliest surviving gas stations anywhere.

361
00:27:34,681 --> 00:27:40,680
Built in 1929, it's been restored to its original glory.

362
00:27:40,681 --> 00:27:45,880
We bought the building and we restored it to what it is today and had lots of help.

363
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:49,100
Again, the city helped us and people donated things.

364
00:27:49,600 --> 00:27:54,380
And so it is probably the most well-known icon on the road today.

365
00:27:55,060 --> 00:28:00,080
Frank Phillips began Phillips Petroleum Company in 1917 in Bartlesville, Oklahoma.

366
00:28:00,760 --> 00:28:04,180
He started marketing gasoline on a retail level.

367
00:28:04,800 --> 00:28:07,500
And he didn't know what to call the gasoline.

368
00:28:07,501 --> 00:28:10,660
So he sent one of his vice presidents to Oklahoma's...

369
00:28:10,680 --> 00:28:14,691
city to pick a name. And they were on Route 66.

370
00:28:14,692 --> 00:28:18,020
And this exact looked over and he said,

371
00:28:18,120 --> 00:28:25,830
we must be doing 60 miles an hour. And the old driver said, 60 hell, we're doing 66.

372
00:28:25,831 --> 00:28:26,840
Doing 66 on

373
00:28:26,841 --> 00:28:27,541
Route 66.

374
00:28:27,542 --> 00:28:32,278
And when he got back to Bartlesville and told Frank Phillips, he said, that's it.

375
00:28:32,279 --> 00:28:32,979
We're

376
00:28:32,980 --> 00:28:34,680
calling it Phillips 66.

377
00:28:34,681 --> 00:28:41,980
Toward the end of the 1930s as America began to recover from the Depression, Europe plunged into war.

378
00:28:42,680 --> 00:28:45,840
Route 66 would soon take on a new identity.

379
00:28:48,180 --> 00:28:54,680
Approximately 80% of original Route 66 roadway survives and is drivable between Chicago and Los Angeles.

380
00:28:55,480 --> 00:28:58,140
Route 66 will return on Modern Marvels.

381
00:29:00,440 --> 00:29:04,440
We now return to Route 66 on Modern Marvels.

382
00:29:04,680 --> 00:29:15,960
Some of the roadway that was originally incorporated into Route 66 back in 1926 is very old.

383
00:29:15,961 --> 00:29:20,420
But this section in New Mexico is positively ancient.

384
00:29:20,421 --> 00:29:25,100
Evidence uncovered by the side of the road here suggests this piece of Route 66 may predate

385
00:29:25,101 --> 00:29:28,700
the earliest known European visitors of the 16th century.

386
00:29:28,701 --> 00:29:32,040
This was a path Native Americans used long before automobiles were invented.

387
00:29:34,680 --> 00:29:37,720
That's a collection right here.

388
00:29:38,920 --> 00:29:41,920
Instead of gas station signs and interstate route shields,

389
00:29:42,360 --> 00:29:45,320
off-roaders can find truly vintage roadside graffiti.

390
00:29:47,820 --> 00:29:51,960
There was a settlement, prehistoric settlement, along the Rio Santa Fe.

391
00:29:52,080 --> 00:29:57,640
For example, there are some housing block remains around here that date to pre-colonial times.

392
00:30:04,680 --> 00:30:15,540
This portion of Route 66, known as the Labahada switchbacks, certainly has the longest history.

393
00:30:15,541 --> 00:30:21,540
It's also the most treacherous, dropping 600 scary feet to the valley below.

394
00:30:21,541 --> 00:30:23,640
It did scare people.

395
00:30:23,641 --> 00:30:30,720
Some of the grades were as great as 22%, which prompted many people to actually have to back up

396
00:30:30,721 --> 00:30:34,660
the road because their gravity-based fuel pumps were unable to draw.

397
00:30:34,680 --> 00:30:41,680
gasoline into the motor if they were going straight ahead.

398
00:30:41,681 --> 00:30:45,680
Crews in the 1920s worked to make the road car-friendly.

399
00:30:45,681 --> 00:30:48,680
This meant carving switchbacks into the hill.

400
00:30:48,681 --> 00:30:50,680
Paving was still out of the question.

401
00:30:50,681 --> 00:30:53,680
They would blast through these solid basalt columns,

402
00:30:53,681 --> 00:30:55,680
and then they would take the rubble,

403
00:30:55,681 --> 00:31:00,680
then build a dry masonry ashlar retaining wall on the outside.

404
00:31:00,681 --> 00:31:03,680
In other words, there was no concrete used.

405
00:31:03,681 --> 00:31:09,180
a curb was usually added above the retaining wall.

406
00:31:09,181 --> 00:31:16,720
In 1932, Route 66 bypassed it with a section of paved road four miles east.

407
00:31:16,721 --> 00:31:23,760
La Bahada soon drifted back into obscurity.

408
00:31:23,761 --> 00:31:30,320
Route 66 stretches across New Mexico's plateaus and mountains, past Santa Rosa through Albuquerque

409
00:31:30,321 --> 00:31:32,560
and on towards Gallup.

410
00:31:32,561 --> 00:31:35,800
A conduit for the homeless and jobless throughout the 1930s.

411
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:41,240
During World War II, Route 66 became important for transporting troops and supplies,

412
00:31:41,700 --> 00:31:42,960
especially here in the Southwest.

413
00:31:43,780 --> 00:31:47,680
Everybody was manufacturing guns and airplanes and tanks and so forth.

414
00:31:48,140 --> 00:31:50,880
So it really was a war road.

415
00:31:51,680 --> 00:31:57,160
Many military bases were positioned adjacent to Route 66 in the deserts of Arizona and California.

416
00:31:59,260 --> 00:32:01,800
But at the time World War II ended,

417
00:32:01,801 --> 00:32:06,300
In 1945, Route 66 really was ready to go.

418
00:32:06,301 --> 00:32:12,300
As the country emerged from war, Route 66 went through another transformation.

419
00:32:12,301 --> 00:32:19,300
From the Honky Talk Road of 1926, through the dust and desperation of 1933,

420
00:32:19,301 --> 00:32:28,300
to the military supply line of 1942, Route 66 would become the neon tourist trap of 1955.

421
00:32:28,301 --> 00:32:31,300
A lot of the GIs who had shipped out of Los Angeles,

422
00:32:31,301 --> 00:32:36,100
Los Angeles and San Diego wanted to come back and vacation there and bring their families there.

423
00:32:36,101 --> 00:32:40,540
So there was a lot of tourism happening throughout the United States at that time.

424
00:32:40,541 --> 00:32:47,480
And really that's when 66 became more of a tourist road than a commercial highway.

425
00:32:47,481 --> 00:32:54,700
Route 66 brought vacationers to the Grand Canyon, the painted desert, and meteor crater in Arizona,

426
00:32:54,701 --> 00:33:00,740
past Winslow and Flagstaff and Seligman and Kingman.

427
00:33:00,741 --> 00:33:02,780
brought them past curio shops and diners,

428
00:33:03,780 --> 00:33:06,960
and a newly born American original, the themed motel.

429
00:33:07,600 --> 00:33:10,920
These roadside shelters evolved from primitive in the 1920s

430
00:33:10,921 --> 00:33:13,500
to almost luxurious in the 1950s.

431
00:33:16,700 --> 00:33:19,860
They went from crude lodges, little cabins.

432
00:33:21,160 --> 00:33:23,040
And then as a business increased,

433
00:33:23,160 --> 00:33:26,140
well, let's get rid of the carport next to it and fill it in.

434
00:33:26,840 --> 00:33:28,720
And you had just total architecture all around.

435
00:33:28,721 --> 00:33:30,620
They even glowed in the dark.

436
00:33:30,740 --> 00:33:43,820
Neon came into play as an inexpensive form of lighting for signage that could be lighted up to attract travelers into their facilities to stay for the night.

437
00:33:44,940 --> 00:33:48,220
Tourists may have been happy, but President Eisenhower was not.

438
00:33:50,720 --> 00:33:56,480
He'd seen Hitler's multi-lane modern high-speed Autobahn as he led Allied forces to Berlin.

439
00:33:56,481 --> 00:34:01,480
Eisenhower wanted America to have its own Autobahn.

440
00:34:01,481 --> 00:34:08,480
After he became president in 1953, he was ready to give it one.

441
00:34:08,481 --> 00:34:16,480
Two-lane roadways were inadequate for the security needs of a Cold War superpower like the United States.

442
00:34:16,481 --> 00:34:24,480
A series of highway bills would send Route 66 itself on the last leg of its journey.

443
00:34:24,481 --> 00:34:29,920
In 1946, Nat King Cole became the first artist to record Bobby Troops classic.

444
00:34:29,921 --> 00:34:32,160
Get your kicks on Route 66.

445
00:34:33,120 --> 00:34:35,520
Route 66 will return on Modern Marvels.

446
00:34:38,720 --> 00:34:42,720
We now return to Route 66 on Modern Marvels.

447
00:34:42,721 --> 00:34:51,920
Seligman, Arizona.

448
00:34:51,921 --> 00:34:53,920
It's the annual Route 66.

449
00:34:53,921 --> 00:34:54,662
66 fun run.

450
00:34:54,664 --> 00:34:59,860
Hundreds of vintage and custom cars spend the weekend driving along surviving

451
00:34:59,861 --> 00:35:01,680
segments of old Route 66.

452
00:35:01,681 --> 00:35:09,180
If you ever plan to motor west travel my way.

453
00:35:09,181 --> 00:35:11,520
It's not a race and no one's in a hurry.

454
00:35:11,521 --> 00:35:17,000
Some come to see the nice cars, some for the weather, some for the friendship and the hospitality.

455
00:35:17,001 --> 00:35:23,900
It's a weekend long event, but Saturday is the high

456
00:35:23,920 --> 00:35:30,920
highlight as a parade of cars drives the 70-mile stretch from Seligman to Kingman.

457
00:35:34,920 --> 00:35:37,920
But parade is the wrong word. It's more of a caravan.

458
00:35:41,920 --> 00:35:46,920
One big, long cookout for fans and friends of Route 66.

459
00:35:47,920 --> 00:35:52,920
It's the moving car show that symbolizes adventure.

